WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.900
How could a few two-choice questions give you the name of a crab?

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.180
You find it on the shore of the Chesapeake Bay, and you do not know what it is.

3
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.320
A key can tell you.

4
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.900
The first film put living things in nested boxes, to show how they are grouped.

5
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.400
This film uses a different tool, called a dichotomous key.

6
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.120
Both tools matter, but they do different jobs.

7
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:33.833
A dichotomous key is a series of two-choice steps about traits you can see.

8
00:00:35.113 --> 00:00:36.833
Each step splits the path in two.

9
00:00:38.053 --> 00:00:40.833
You follow the choice that fits, until you reach a name.

10
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:45.717
Our key holds seven animals, each from a different phylum.

11
00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:49.197
Step one asks, fins or no fins?

12
00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:52.717
The crab has no fins, so we go on.

13
00:00:53.937 --> 00:00:57.037
Step two asks, jointed legs or no jointed legs?

14
00:00:57.777 --> 00:00:59.757
The crab has jointed legs.

15
00:01:00.817 --> 00:01:04.917
The path ends at a name, Callinectes sapidus, the blue crab.

16
00:01:06.197 --> 00:01:07.197
Look at the whole key.

17
00:01:08.457 --> 00:01:12.257
The trout comes off the path first, the crab second, and the oyster third.

18
00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:16.347
Now keep every question, but change their order.

19
00:01:17.707 --> 00:01:21.587
Ask about the two-part shell and the five arms before the jointed legs.

20
00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:24.267
Watch the branches redraw.

21
00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:26.657
Run the crab again.

22
00:01:27.897 --> 00:01:29.197
It takes more steps now.

23
00:01:30.297 --> 00:01:33.877
But the path ends at the same name, Callinectes sapidus.

24
00:01:35.057 --> 00:01:36.637
Every animal keeps its name.

25
00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:39.177
Only its neighbors change.

26
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.887
The crab now sits right next to the sea star.

27
00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:47.147
Has the crab become more closely related to the sea star?

28
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.540
No. Drop the animals into the boxes from the first film.

29
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.780
The crab is in phylum Arthropoda.

30
00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.060
The sea star and the oyster are each in a different phylum.

31
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.740
The crab shares only two boxes with each of them, the domain and the kingdom.

32
00:02:12.883 --> 00:02:15.683
The crab shares just two boxes with the trout, too.

33
00:02:16.963 --> 00:02:19.223
In either order, the boxes stay the same.

34
00:02:20.503 --> 00:02:23.803
Moving a question cannot make two animals closer relatives.

35
00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:26.253
Why does this happen?

36
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:31.373
This key asks about traits that are easy to see, not who is related.

37
00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:35.293
So its order alone does not show who is related.

38
00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:39.473
The boxes show how one classification groups them.

39
00:02:40.613 --> 00:02:45.913
To draw the boxes, scientists compare body parts, what living things do, and DNA.
